<p class="eyebrow">Barbershop style</p>
<h1 id="page-title" class="h1">What makes a chord ring?</h1>
<p class="lede">Barbershop is a uniquely American style of a cappella singing: four voices, no instruments, close harmony on nearly every melody note. When those four voices tune precisely, their overtones reinforce one another and the chord seems to “ring” — many listeners hear a note that no one is actually singing.</p>
</div>
<figure class="stack-figure" aria-labelledby="stack-cap">
<ol>
<li class="p-tenor"><span>Tenor</span> <span>harmony above the melody</span></li>
<li class="p-lead"><span>Lead</span> <span>sings the melody</span></li>
<li class="p-bari"><span>Baritone</span> <span>completes the chord</span></li>
<li class="p-bass"><span>Bass</span> <span>the foundation</span></li>
</ol>
<figcaption id="stack-cap">The four parts, highest to lowest.</figcaption>
</figure>
</div>
</section>

<section class="section" aria-labelledby="parts-title">
<div class="wrap stack parts">
<div class="parts__intro stack">
<h2 id="parts-title" class="h2">The four parts</h2>
<p class="muted">Women's quartets use the same part names as men's — tenor, lead, baritone and bass — with every part voiced in women's ranges. The arrangement, not the singer, sets the rules.</p>
</div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="part">
<h3>Lead</h3>
<p class="muted">Usually carries the melody and sets the emotional delivery of the song. The other three parts tune to the lead.</p>
</div>
<div class="part">
<h3>Tenor</h3>
<p class="muted">Harmonizes above the melody, typically the highest voice, sung with a lighter, brighter tone so it sparkles over the chord.</p>
</div>
<div class="part">
<h3>Baritone</h3>
<p class="muted">Fills in whichever note completes the chord, crossing above and below the lead — often the trickiest part to learn.</p>
</div>
<div class="part">
<h3>Bass</h3>
<p class="muted">The lowest voice, singing mostly roots and fifths. A solid, well-tuned bass gives the other three something to lock onto.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>

<section class="section section--sand hallmarks" aria-labelledby="sound-title">
<div class="wrap split">
<div class="hallmarks__intro stack">
<h2 id="sound-title" class="h2">How to recognize it</h2>
<p class="muted">Listen for these hallmarks in any of our <a href="/videos/">videos</a>.</p>
</div>
<dl class="grid">
<div><dt>The barbershop seventh</dt><dd>The signature chord — a dominant seventh — appears far more often here than in other styles.</dd></div>
<div><dt>Lock and ring</dt><dd>Singers tune by ear rather than to a piano, so chords lock in place and produce audible overtones.</dd></div>
<div><dt>Swipes</dt><dd>The chord changes underneath a single held word or syllable.</dd></div>
<div><dt>Tags</dt><dd>A dramatic ending, often with one part holding a long note while the others move around it.</dd></div>
<div><dt>One voice, four parts</dt><dd>All four parts usually sing the same words at the same time, so the lyrics stay clear.</dd></div>
<div><dt>Woodshedding</dt><dd>Making up harmony by ear, without sheet music — the way barbershop began.</dd></div>
</dl>
</div>
</section>

<section class="section history" aria-labelledby="history-title">
<div class="wrap stack">
<h2 id="history-title" class="h2">A short history</h2>
<p class="muted">Barbershop harmony grew out of informal close-harmony singing in late-1800s America, with deep roots in African American vocal traditions. Neighborhood barbershops were gathering places where men harmonized while they waited — and the name stuck.</p>
<p class="muted">The style was revived and preserved by singing societies in the twentieth century. Women formed organizations of their own, including Harmony, Inc., founded in 1959 and still home to women's quartets and choruses across North America — High Gear among them.</p>
<p><a href="/high-societies/" class="text-link">Learn about Harmony, Inc.</a></p>
</div>
</section>
